  "Number": 10,
  "Question": "The statement \\(x^2-x-6\\lt0\\) is equivalent to the statement:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-2\\lt x\\lt3\\)",
    "B": "\\(x\\gt-2\\)",
    "C": "\\(x\\lt3\\)",
    "D": "\\(x\\gt3\\) and \\(x\\lt-2\\)",
    "E": "\\(x\\gt3\\) or \\(x\\lt-2\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "Factoring makes the sign of the expression easier to track. Since \\(2+(-3)=-1\\) and \\(2(-3)=-6\\),\n\\[\nx^2-x-6=(x-3)(x+2).\n\\]\nA product is negative exactly when its factors have opposite signs. The arrangement\n\\[\nx-3<0,\n\\qquad x+2>0\n\\]\nrequires \\(x<3\\) and \\(x>-2\\), which together give \\(-2<x<3\\).\n\nThe other opposite-sign arrangement would require both \\(x>3\\) and \\(x<-2\\), which is impossible. Equal-sign factors give a positive product, and at \\(x=-2\\) or \\(x=3\\) the product is zero. Thus all and only the solutions are \\(-2<x<3\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{A}\\).",
